+## 17. X2 task T2: `profile lint` (2026-10-08)
+
+New subcommand `heimdall-proto profile lint --file <path> [--json]` (TASK.md). Nothing else changed in
+behaviour; `profile show` and every other command produce the same output as before.
+
+### Design
+
+- **Input handling is `profile show`'s own code path.** `main.rs` calls the same `profile::load_file`, prints
+  the same stderr line and the same error envelope, and returns the same exit code. So SCHEMA_INVALID,
+  INPUT_TOO_LARGE, USAGE_ERROR for missing files and directories, K2, K14 (FIFO, socket, device: O_NONBLOCK open
+  plus regular-file check), K15 and K18 behave identically. A CLI test compares lint and show byte for byte
+  (stdout, stderr, exit) on every invalid fixture, an oversize file, a K2 control-character file, a directory and
+  `/dev/null`, with `--json` first, last and absent.
+- **Argument parsing** reuses `parse_opts` (`--file` value flag plus `--json`, in any order). Missing `--file`,
+  a flag without its value, an unknown flag or a repeated flag exits 2, with a USAGE_ERROR JSON result on
+  stdout only when `--json` is present (§1).
+- **Rules** live in the library module `src/lint.rs`. Each rule is one pass with a hash map. Findings are compact
+  `(destination index, field)` places. Rule order and the order inside each rule follow TASK.md. Rule 1 groups by
+  (ASCII-lowercased host, effective port). Rule 4 groups exact label strings within one destination. Rule 5
+  groups all labels by their ASCII-lowercased form and reports a group only when it holds two or more different
+  spellings. ASCII lowercasing is `str::to_ascii_lowercase`, so non-ASCII letters (`Ü`, `ß`, the Kelvin sign) are
+  never folded. `destination_ids` is derived from the places. Every rule emits places in non-decreasing
+  destination order, so skipping a repeat of the previous index removes every repeat.
+- **Output.** `LINT_CLEAN` exits 0 and `LINT_FINDINGS` exits 3; both are new `Code` variants with
+  `state: succeeded`. `schemas/` was not edited, as TASK.md requires. The `--json` line is written straight into
+  one buffer (`lint::json_line`), not built as a `serde_json::Value` tree. The first version built the tree, and
+  its peak RSS on a 1 MiB file with 262k labels was 187 MiB on darwin, against 95 MiB for `profile show` on the
+  same file. The direct writer measures 116 MiB on darwin. On linux-arm64 it measures 54 MiB, against 68 MiB for
+  `profile show`. A unit test asserts that `json_line` is byte-identical to `Envelope::success(..).to_line()` of
+  the same data, including ids that need JSON escaping. The two binaries also gave byte-identical output on the
+  worst-case files and on `large-100.toml`.
+- **Value non-echo.** Findings carry key paths built from fixed ASCII and indexes, fixed messages and destination
+  ids only. Output without `--json` (K12: not judged) prints `rule: message (key paths)` lines and a count,
+  without ids. Closed stdout is ignored like `emit`, so the wait status is the same with and without `--json`.
+- **No writes.** Lint only opens the profile read-only. A CLI test snapshots the profile's directory tree before
+  and after.
+
+### Tests added
+
+- `src/lint.rs` (15 unit tests): every rule with its near misses. These cover port 22, omitted ports, 1023 and
+  1024; `Root`, `ROOT` and `root ` are not `root`; the same label in two destinations is not a finding; case
+  variants inside one destination are rule 5 and not rule 4; ASCII-only folding; combined rule 4 and rule 5; rule
+  order; exactly four members per finding and `data` holding only `findings`; the byte-identity check against
+  `Envelope`; value non-echo; 100k labels linted in under 2 s.
+- `src/cli.rs`: lint parsing in both option orders, and lint usage errors.
+- `tests/cli.rs` (8 black-box tests):
+  - an exact-JSON all-rules profile at a path with spaces, `ü` and `東京`, in both option orders;
+  - exit 3 without `--json`;
+  - no profile value on stdout or stderr in any mode;
+  - clean fixtures;
+  - `large-100.toml`, whose every third destination has account `root`, so lint reports 33 root-account
+    findings;
+  - byte-for-byte equivalence with `profile show` on invalid and non-regular inputs;
+  - K14 within 2 s, covering a FIFO, a directory, `/dev/null` and a socket;
+  - usage errors;
+  - K12 closed-stdout parity.
+- Outside the crate, for this task only (`<scratch>/ref_lint.py`): an independent Python reference of the
+  TASK.md rules was run differentially on 3,000 random profiles. The profiles drew on colliding hosts, labels,
+  accounts and ports, including non-ASCII spellings. The darwin-arm64 artifact had 0 mismatches. The
+  linux-arm64 artifact, run in the harness image with `--network none`, also had 0 mismatches.
+
+### Results (all real exit codes)
+
+- `cargo fmt --check` 0; `cargo clippy --locked --all-targets -- -D warnings` 0 for the host and 0 for
+  `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`.
+- `cargo test --locked` on darwin-arm64: exit 0. Lib 64, bin 4 and cli 36 passed; `tests/windows.rs` has 0 tests
+  on Unix.
+- `./build.sh`: exit 0, and all four targets built (darwin-arm64, linux-x64, linux-arm64, windows-x64).
+- The cli tests against `dist/darwin-arm64/heimdall-proto`, with no toolchain on PATH, exited 0 with 36 passed.
+- The cross-built linux-arm64 tests ran in the pinned Debian container against `dist/linux-arm64` and exited 0.
+  Lib 64, bin 4 and cli 36 passed, and 1 test (`linux_nproc`) is ignored by design.
+- Harness 2.4.2, run read-only from the workspace:
+
+| Platform | Command | Exit | Checks | Status / verdict |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| darwin-arm64 | `tests/g02/.venv/bin/python tests/g02/conformance.py --bin dist/darwin-arm64/heimdall-proto` | 0 | 139/139 | `verified` / `pass` |
+| linux-arm64 | `sh tests/g02/linux/run.sh --artifact-dir dist/linux-arm64` | 0 | 139/139 | `verified` / `pass` |
+
+### Known gaps
+
+- No independent review of this change was available in this run. The Python reference is a second
+  implementation by the same author, not an independent reading of TASK.md.
+- TASK.md does not say whether `destination_ids` may appear in output without `--json`. They are omitted there,
+  which is the conservative reading.
+- The new codes are absent from `schemas/g02/result.schema.json` (by instruction). A strict schema validation of
+  a lint result therefore fails on `code`.
+- Windows: `profile lint` shares `profile show`'s Windows input path (GetFileType check, §9.6), but it was only
+  compile-checked (clippy for `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`, `build.sh` windows-x64). No native Windows run was made.
